Found in k73. getInt in cmd/launcher/helpers.go turns a float64 argument into int with int(v): no range or integrality check. offset=1e20 becomes MinInt64, so read_file reads from the start instead of refusing, and limit=1e20 reports "got -9223372036854775808". Non-integral values (1.5) are truncated silently. Fix: refuse a number that is not integral or does not fit an int, naming the argument; one test per case. Touches helpers.go, so it runs after k119 lands.

Fix in cmd/launcher/helpers.go getInt: a float64 must be integral (v == math.Trunc(v), which also refuses NaN) and lie in [MinInt, 2^63); the upper bound is -float64(math.MinInt) because float64(math.MaxInt) rounds up to 2^63. Errors, verbatim: "'offset' must be an integer, got 1.5" and "'offset' must be an integer between -9223372036854775808 and 9223372036854775807, got 1e+20". My choice beyond the card: a numeric string that overflows (strconv.ErrRange) gets the same range message, so the same value reads alike whether a client sends it as number or string (before: "must be an integer, got ..."). Callers read_file offset/limit and logs lines all return errResult(err), so the style is unchanged without touching filetools.go or tools.go. Tests in helpers_test.go: TestGetIntRefusesNonIntegralAndOutOfRange (non-integral, too large, first value past MaxInt, too small, overflowing string; all red before the fix), TestGetIntAcceptsIntegralEdges (negative offset, 0, MinInt; an off-by-one lower-bound mutant turns it red), TestReadFileRefusesOffsetBeyondInt (end to end through JSON-RPC; before the fix read_file returned the whole file).

